        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Thanks for taking a look.  Yeah, this is from a real traced cost I came across this when working on improving text input speed in a neovim client for Obsidian.  I'll trim back the tests.\r\n\r\nAn LLM wrote some benchmarks for me, exercising some different nvim actions in a markdown file while having activated the plugin render-markdown.nvim .  I ran them a number of times in various ways, trying to eliminate noise, etc.\r\n\r\nHere's the most interesting table.  In particular, note the significant regression in navigation.\r\n\r\nOperation | Change at 350 ms pacing | Change at 700 ms pacing\r\n-- | -- | --\r\nInitial file opening | 6.9% less CPU | 6.5% less CPU\r\nEditing | 13.1% less CPU | 18.7% less CPU\r\nUndo | 17.0% less CPU | 19.6% less CPU\r\nScrolling | 22.8% less CPU | 22.7% less CPU\r\nNavigation | 80.2% **more** CPU | 72.1% **more** CPU\r\n\r\nI'm aiming it at the navigation issue now and changing the PR back to a draft until that regression is eliminated.",
